<h2>The petition and its single ground</h2>
<p>The petitioners sought quashing of a 2021 FIR under Sections 498A, 406 and 34 IPC. Their argument rested entirely on one proposition: the marriage between the first petitioner and the complainant was void, and Supreme Court authority holds that a conviction under Section 498A cannot stand where there is no valid marriage. On that footing, they said, the prosecution was stillborn.</p>

<h2>Why the Court was not persuaded</h2>
<p>Justice Girish Kathpalia tested the argument against the FIR as a whole, and three things stood in its way.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The FIR alleged more than cruelty.</strong> It contained allegations of assault and of a forcible miscarriage, and an allegation of cheating: that the marriage was solemnised on the representation that divorce proceedings from the petitioner&rsquo;s earlier marriage had been instituted, a representation later found to be false. Prima facie, offences beyond Section 498A appeared from the FIR itself.</div>
    <div class="fstep"><strong>The void-marriage plea cuts both ways.</strong> The very fact said to make the marriage void — the subsisting earlier marriage, concealed by a false representation — is the foundation of the cheating allegation. An argument designed to escape Section 498A thus reinforced another part of the prosecution case.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The charge stage was the proper forum.</strong> Charges had not been framed. The trial court is bound to consider at that stage which offences are made out, uninfluenced by observations in the quashing proceedings, and the petitioners retain every remedy there.</div>
</div>
<p>Finding the petition devoid of merit and apparently filed for oblique purposes, the Court dismissed it with costs of Rs 20,000, to be deposited within a week, and directed that a copy of the judgment be sent to the trial court.</p>

<h2>The place of the void-marriage defence after this decision</h2>
<p>The decision does not reject the void-marriage defence as a matter of law; it polices where and how the defence is raised. Where Section 498A is the only offence and the invalidity of the marriage is apparent from undisputed documents, the argument can have real force. But quashing under the inherent power requires that the FIR, taken at its face, discloses no offence at all. An FIR that also alleges assault, miscarriage caused without consent, and inducement into the marriage itself will survive the excision of Section 498A, and the petition will fail — now, in Delhi, often with costs.</p>

<h2>Practical notes for both sides</h2>
<p>For accused persons: a quashing petition should be weighed against the charge-stage remedy. Where the grounds are arguments on the legal ingredients of offences rather than a demonstration that the FIR is inherently absurd or mala fide, the charge hearing before the Magistrate or Sessions Court is usually the better forum — it carries no risk of a costs order branding the plea frivolous, and a discharge there is a decision on merits. For complainants: the decision underlines the value of a first information report that states every injury and inducement with particularity, because the breadth of the FIR is precisely what defeats single-ground quashing petitions.</p>
<div class="note">For conduct after 1 July 2024, Section 498A IPC corresponds to Sections 85 and 86 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, Section 406 IPC to Section 316 BNS, and the inherent power under Section 482 CrPC to Section 528 of the BNSS, 2023. This FIR, registered in 2021, continues under the IPC. Nothing in this article is advice in any pending case.</div>
